WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
Bash does not simply pass a source line directly to an external program.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:20.040
It parses shell syntax, expands variables, processes substitutions, performs word splitting, expands wildcard patterns, handles redirection, sets up pipelines, and then starts a command with a resulting argument list.

3
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.220
That transformation is why command construction is security-relevant.

4
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.380
The command a reviewer sees in a script is not always the same thing an external tool receives.

5
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.720
A variable can expand to a value that becomes one argument or several.

6
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.860
Pattern-like text can be expanded into pathnames.

7
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.840
Redirection changes where input and output go.

8
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.260
A pipeline can make one command's result drive another command's behavior.

9
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.300
Argument boundaries are central.

10
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.320
A command receives a list of arguments, not a vague sentence of text.

11
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:56.420
When a script preserves boundaries, a file name remains a file name and a mode remains a mode.

12
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.760
When a script collapses everything into a command string, Bash has another opportunity to interpret the text as syntax.

13
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:13.520
Command substitution is useful when a script intentionally captures a value from another command, but it should be kept conceptually separate from dynamic command construction.

14
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.380
The defensive question is not whether Bash can evaluate something.

15
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.480
It is whether the script author intended that evaluation and whether a reviewer can see the boundary clearly.

16
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.040
Word splitting and globbing are frequent sources of surprise.

17
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.940
They can change how many arguments a command receives and what those arguments contain.

18
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:39.640
Quoting, arrays, and explicit validation are practical ways to keep values stable as they move from input to execution.

19
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.280
For prevention, think in terms of structured execution.

20
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.940
The script should choose a known command path, build a known argument list, and pass values in positions where they remain data.

21
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.480
Avoid designs that require Bash to reinterpret generated text as a fresh command line.

22
00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:02.060
Review confirms that every shell evaluation is intended and that each data-to-syntax boundary is visible.
